pouch boundary-line patch 0012 -- a failed mallocng assert is abort().

mallocng's internal `assert` macro (glue.h; distinct from libc's assert,
which already reaches abort()) ends the program with a_crash(), a deliberate
NULL store. It guards the allocator's hot path -- alloc_slot::enframe's
assert(!p[-4]), get_nominal_size's assert(!end[-5]) and about ten more -- so
it is what a heap corruption looks like when mallocng catches it.

History. Phase 6 hardening item #3b first swapped a_crash() for _Exit(127):
the NULL store then extincted the kernel, so every detected corruption took
the boot down. Faults have long since ended only the offending Proc, and
gcpark-b (2026-10-12, operator vote "snare:abort") makes abort() one trap the
kernel names (0011-pouch-abort.patch). This patch now calls abort():

  - the parent reads WIFSIGNALED with WTERMSIG == SIGABRT, and the console
    prints `user fault: ... reason="snare:abort" ... pc=... lr=...`, the pc
    inside abort() and the lr inside the allocator function whose assert
    failed -- the location upstream's a_crash() would also have given, but
    named as an abort rather than read as a stray SIGSEGV;
  - abort() is one brk instruction, async-signal-safe and independent of any
    allocator state, as a_crash() is, so it is safe under the malloc lock.

The assert stays content-free (no message). POUCH_MALLOCNG_DIAG
(0013-pouch-mallocng-diag.patch) adds an opt-in dump before the enframe
assertion fires.

Threads. A fault in any thread ends the whole Proc (I-24), so an assert in
any thread is sound; the old multi-thread hazard of this patch was closed by
#811.

--- a/src/malloc/mallocng/glue.h
+++ b/src/malloc/mallocng/glue.h
@@ -1,6 +1,7 @@
 #ifndef MALLOC_GLUE_H
 #define MALLOC_GLUE_H

+#include <stdlib.h>
 #include <stdint.h>
 #include <sys/mman.h>
 #include <pthread.h>
@@ -29,8 +30,13 @@
 #if USE_REAL_ASSERT
 #include <assert.h>
 #else
 #undef assert
-#define assert(x) do { if (!(x)) a_crash(); } while(0)
+/* Pouch override: a failed mallocng assert is abort() -- one brk the kernel
+ * names snare:abort (0011-pouch-abort.patch) -- so the parent reads SIGABRT
+ * and the console names the failing pc, where a_crash()'s NULL store would
+ * read as a stray SIGSEGV. Like a_crash(), abort() touches no allocator
+ * state, so it is safe under the malloc lock. */
+#define assert(x) do { if (!(x)) abort(); } while(0)
 #endif

 #define brk(p) ((uintptr_t)__syscall(SYS_brk, p))
